# pjproject's bundled libsrtp is statically linked into
# libcomlink_core, so lintian correctly sees an embedded copy.
#
# This is deliberate, not an oversight. Both supported platforms must
# build from one pinned pjproject revision: Android has no system
# libsrtp, so linking Debian's libsrtp2 here would leave the two
# frontends running different SRTP implementations. For a phone whose
# whole premise is identical behaviour on both platforms, divergence in
# the media security path is the worst place to accept it.
#
# The cost is accepted knowingly: a libsrtp security advisory requires
# this package to be rebuilt rather than picking up a system update.
# Whoever maintains this must watch libsrtp advisories directly.
comlink: embedded-library srtp [usr/lib/*/libcomlink_core.so.*]

# One package holds the application, the shared library and the C ABI
# header. That is not the usual Debian library split, and lintian is
# right to notice.
#
# Deliberate: the library has exactly two consumers and both ship in
# this package, so splitting it produced three packages to install and
# bought nothing. The header comes along because the Android port
# builds against the same ABI and having it is free.
comlink: package-name-doesnt-match-sonames
comlink: shared-library-lacks-prerequisites
comlink: sharedobject-in-library-directory-missing-soname
